,title,link,text,language,family
0,Austroasiatic languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austroasiatic_languages,"The Austroasiatic languages are a large language family spoken throughout Mainland Southeast Asia, South Asia, and East Asia. These languages are natively spoken by the majority of the population in Vietnam and Cambodia...",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
1,"Austroasiatic languages - Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia",http://bluecomtech.com/Web+Sites/3D+Printer+List/en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Austroasiatic_languages.html,"The Austroasiatic languages, in recent classifications synonymous with Mon–Khmer, are a large language family of continental Southeast Asia, also scattered throughout India, Bangladesh, and the southern border of China.",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
2,"Austroasiatic languages | Mon-Khmer, Munda & Vietic | Britannica",https://www.britannica.com/topic/Austroasiatic-languages,"Austroasiatic languages, stock of some 150 languages spoken by more than 65 million people scattered throughout Southeast Asia and eastern India. Most of these languages have numerous dialects.",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
3,O2a Haplogroup: Austro-Asiatic DNA in India | Helixline,https://helixline.in/blog/o2a-haplogroup-india,The Austro-Asiatic language family is one of the oldest and most widespread language families in Asia. It includes approximately 150 languages spoken by over 100 million people across South and Southeast Asia. The two major branches relevant to our discussion are,Mon,Austro-Asiatic
4,Austroasiatic languages Facts for Kids,https://kids.kiddle.co/Austroasiatic_languages,The Austroasiatic languages are a large family of languages. They are mainly spoken in Southeast Asia and parts of India. There are about 157 different Austroasiatic languages. Around 117 million people speak them.,Mon,Austro-Asiatic
5,[Solved] Mon-Khmer language group belongs to,https://testbook.com/question-answer/mon-khmer-language-group-belongs-to-__________--67fdf3796195f3ec9fa70992,"The Mon-Khmer language group is a major subgroup of the Austro-Asiatic language family, which is predominantly spoken in Southeast Asia. This language group includes languages such as Khmer (spoken in Cambodia) and Vietnamese.",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
6,Austro-Asiatic language - definition of Austro-Asiatic language by...,https://www.thefreedictionary.com/Austro-Asiatic+language,"Austro-Asiatic, Munda-Mon-Khmer. natural language, tongue - a human written or spoken language used by a community; opposed to e.g. a computer language. Munda - a family of languages spoken by people scattered throughout central India.",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
7,'austro-asiatic' related words: munda-mon-khmer [5 more],https://relatedwords.org/relatedto/austro-asiatic,"Here are some words that are associated with austro-asiatic: munda-mon-khmer, mon-khmer, munda, tongue, austro-asiatic language, natural language. You can get the definitions of these austro-asiatic related words by clicking on them.",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
8,Austro-Asiatic etymology : Query result,https://starlingdb.org/cgi-bin/response.cgi?root=config&basename=/data/aas/aaet&first=141,Proto-Austro-Asiatic: cVŋ. Meaning: gong. Austric etymology: Austric etymology. Thai: chiŋ.B musical instrument. Proto-Bahnaric: (loan). Khmer: chɨŋ < OK cheŋ.,Mon,Austro-Asiatic
9,Austro-Asiatic Language Family - Structure & Dialects - MustGo,https://www-mustgo-com.nproxy.org/worldlanguages/austro-asiatic-language-family/,"The Austro-Asiatic language family consists of 169 languages spoken in Southeast Asia, in countries located between China and Indonesia. A few are spoken to the west of this area in the Nicobar Islands and in India. The austro– part of the name comes from the Latin word for ‘south.’",Mon,Austro-Asiatic
